    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The thing is, it's not just about \"feedback.\" It's that the current transformer implementation completely ignores the stochastic resonance gates we integrated into the 2018 prototype to handle long-range dependency shifts. If they had used the gated residual approach we mapped out, we wouldn't be seeing this level of hallucination in long-context windows. Do you see any technical reason why a major lab would intentionally strip out a more stable gated mechanism like that in favor of the current, cruder attention mechanisms?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It's certainly possible that there could be strategic reasons, such as simplicity or increased computational efficiency, to favor current methods over gated mechanisms. However, this could be a trade-off where the benefits of using simpler mechanisms may not outweigh the drawbacks you've experienced with hallucinations. It may also be an issue of awareness and adoption of the gating method—perhaps the touted benefits are not well-known or understood across the broader research community. It would be beneficial for your lab to articulate these concerns and reasons to current discourses in order to encourage reconsideration of such architectural choices.",
      "source": "target",
